Props are properties used to pass data from a parent component to a child component. Props are read-only from the child component's perspective.

State is data managed inside a component that can change over time. I use useState to create and update state, and when the state changes, React re-renders the component.

Prop drilling happens when data is passed from a parent component to a deeply nested child through multiple intermediate components using props, even though those intermediate components don't need the data.

For example, if App has logged-in user data and I pass it to Dashboard, then Layout, and finally Navbar just so Navbar can use it, that is prop drilling.

To avoid this when the same data is needed by multiple components, I can use Context API. In my project, I use Context API to share logged-in user information across components like Navbar, UserProfile, and Dashboard.

Props = direct parent → child
Prop drilling = parent → child → child → child just to pass data
Context = shared data accessible to descendants without passing it through every level.

